  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1200 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nPattern remains in place through Tuesday and Wednesday, with high\npressure stalling over the Appalachians and low pressure\ncontinuing to deepen in the lee of the northern Rockies. Highs in\nthe mid-50s and gusty southwest winds are expected both days, \nwith cloud cover increasing on Wednesday. \n\nLow pressure finally ejects from the northern High Plains\nWednesday afternoon, approaching the Upper Midwest Wednesday\nnight. Modeling still indicates plenty of dry air near the surface\neven with southwesterly winds, combined with ensemble agreement \nthat the center of the low will sweep northeastward through \nMinnesota into Ontario. This indicates that any developing\nrain/showers will remain light. Kept NBM PoPs of ~15% overnight,\nwith 20-30% chances into Thursday as low pressure enters Ontario.\nWinds shift westerly behind a cold front Thursday night and\nquickly diminish as high pressure briefly ridges back into the \nregion.\n\nFriday, low pressure deepens in the central High Plains. This \nbrings increasing moisture and southeasterly winds to the Upper \nMidwest, as well as mid-level WAA/warm frontogenesis. Expect highs\nin the upper 50s to perhaps the low 60s, with frontogenesis\nbringing chances for precipitation to the Wisconsin River Valley\nand into central Wisconsin through the day Friday (15-25%). Friday\nnight, WAA continues as low pressure ejects into the Plains and\nshowers become more widespread (30-60% chances). Ensemble \nmodeling shows a wide spread of potential low locations going into\nSaturday due to the strength of the lingering high over the \nAppalachians. This high may serve to strengthen the low as it \npivots into the Plains (GFS and Canadian solutions), bringing \ngusty southerly winds and potential for even some severe weather \nto southern Wisconsin. However, Euro is holding to a broader area\nof weaker low pressure, keeping winds less intense and keeping\nsouthern Wisconsin in more broad rainfall with isolated embedded\nthunderstorm cells. Ensembles show very weak instability\npotential, with only a few members indicating over 500 J/kg of\nCAPE. Keep an eye on the forecast as adjustments are made as the\nevent approaches. Regardless of low placement, confidence is high\nin widespread rainfall (50-80%) Friday night through Saturday.\nWith steady warm advection, expect highs in the low 60s possible \non Saturday.\n\nLow pressure looks to linger, gradually lifting northeastward\nthrough Sunday, bringing additional rounds of showers (20-40%\nchances). Precipitation exits along a cold front Sunday, with \nwinds becoming northwesterly and gusty. Temperatures return to\nnear-normal behind the front.\n\nMH",
